  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon reveals a well defined upper\nlow in SK/MB, with its southern flank and dry slot over ND. This\nis contributing to gusty southwest winds, including 50 mph gusts\nin northeast ND this afternoon. This upper low migrates eastward\nTuesday, keeping breezy winds in the forecast Tuesday afternoon.\n\nDrier, post-frontal air mass behind last night's cold front will\nremain in place over our area Tuesday into Wednesday. Calm,\ncool, and likely mostly clear conditions in addition to recent\nrainfall will allow potential for fog Tuesday night/Wednesday \nmorning.\n\nInto mid week around Wednesday, upper ridging begins to build \ninto the Northern Plains downstream of a longwave upper trough\ndigging into the Pacific Coast. This will promote moisture\nreturn into the Northern Plains and Upper Midwest late Wednesday\nand Thursday. This continues as upper ridging further builds\ninto the Plains in response to the digging and non-progressive\nupper troughing in the West. This will several days of southwest\nflow aloft over our region allowing for continued and somewhat\nlengthy period of moisture and heat return through late week\ninto the weekend. This will bring ingredients into the Northern\nPlains and Upper Midwest for potential strong to severe storms\nas well as late summer heat with potential for minor heat \nrelated impacts.\n\nEnsemble guidance suggests the upper trough and ridge \nwave packet breaks down into early next week, which typically\nwould signal the end of heat and severe potential. However,\nthere are notable difference between ensemble systems in how\nthis breakdown evolves - thus confidence in the extended \nforecast is low.\n\n...Gusty winds this afternoon...\n\nA tightened surface pressure gradient collocated with \nrelatively deep mixing in northeast ND is allowing for southwest\nwind gusts 40-50 mph. This is expected to continue through the \nafternoon until around sunset when deep daytime mixing begins to\ncease. Wind gusts will rapidly dwindle, although breezy winds \n10-15 mph are expected to continue through the overnight. While \n40-50 mph gusts are not typically impactful for northeast ND, \nseasonal factors like full foliage on trees, as well as this \narea not experiencing these longer duration high of gusts in \nover a month or two will contribute to bring low impacts from \ngusty winds. A Wind Advisory will remain in effect until 7 PM \nfor this area.\n\n\n...Potential strong to severe storms mid to late this week...\n\nStarting late Wednesday into Thursday, several model guidance\nsystems bring one or more shortwave impulses within the initial\nsurge of southwest flow aloft as the upper ridge brings to\nbuild. This also houses increasing overall moisture that \nremains pooled in the southern half of the CONUS along with\nsteepening lapse rates aloft sourced from the Central Rockies.\nGuidance brings low but sufficient parameter spacing with\nrespect to instability and shear late Wednesday to early \nThursday timeframe. This lends some credence in strong to severe\nstorms being more on the lower end of severity, as well as\nlikelihood of storms being elevated bringing hail to the\nforefront of potential hazards.\n\nOverall, there are no glaring signals for significant severe \nweather despite there being several opportunities for \ningredients for severe weather to come together mid to late this\nweek. This is backed by ML/AI guidance highlighting our region \nlate Wednesday through Saturday for severe potential.\n\nIt is worth noting this pattern could result in locally heavy\nrain and potential for flash flooding given the longer duration\nof moisture flow and an upper pattern that is non-progressive\npotentially allowing for several shortwave impulses to traverse\nthrough our region. Confidence in this is low, however, as there\nare unknown mesoscale influences that are necessary for flash\nflooding yet to be correctly resolved by modeled guidance.\n\n...Late summer-like heat this weekend...\n\nEnsemble guidance strongly suggests in strong upper ridging into\nthe Upper Midwest this weekend. This will promote increasing\ntemperatures aloft, as well as the migration and pooling of\nmoisture near the surface. This will not only allow opportunity\nfor 90s back into the forecast, but also high humidity. As of\nnow, Friday and Saturday look to be the days where these two \nmay align to bring heat indices well into the 90s, and perhaps \ninto the low 100s (10 percent chance of occurring). While \nSaturday looks to be hottest within the forecast period, this \ncould also extend into Sunday, although confidence is low in \nthis.",
